---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  APPLICATIONS,
  APPLICATION_INDUSTRIES,
  APPLICATION_PART_FAMILIES,
  APPLICATION_MATRIX,
} from '../../data/applications';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

// Applications coverage map (rebuild B3). This page renders the planning
// matrix itself — industry x part family — from applications.ts. It is
// deliberately honest about coverage: a cell is either a published guide
// (linked), in the coverage plan (candidate), or not in the plan at all.
// Nothing here is a page that does not exist.
const cellFor = (industry: string, partFamily: string) =>
  APPLICATION_MATRIX.find((c) => c.industry === industry && c.partFamily === partFamily);
const appFor = (industry: string, partFamily: string) =>
  APPLICATIONS.find((a) => a.industry === industry && a.partFamily === partFamily);
const publishedCount = APPLICATION_MATRIX.filter((c) => c.status === 'published').length;
const plannedCount = APPLICATION_MATRIX.filter((c) => c.status !== 'published').length;
// Industries with a live hub page (the hub routes are built per industry).
const HUB_INDUSTRIES = new Set(APPLICATIONS.map((a) => a.industry));
---

<Base
  title="Nylon 3D Printing Applications — Coverage Map | Nylon3DP"
  description="The coverage map of the Nylon3DP application library: which industry and part-family combinations have published guides, which are planned, and which are not."
>
  <div class="hub-band">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Applications</p>
    <h1>Applications of nylon 3D printing</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <p class="qa-label">Quick answer</p>
    <p>This page is the coverage map of the application library — the matrix the library is built from. Each cell is an industry paired with a part family. {publishedCount} cells have published guides today (the <a href="/applications/drones/">Drones &amp; UAV hub</a>), {plannedCount} are in the coverage plan, and the rest are not planned. A guide is published only when it passes a double gate: real demand for the question, and a complete part-specific kernel — selection reasoning, its own design numbers, failure modes and economics. No cell becomes a page to fill the grid.</p>
  </div>

  <p>There are thousands of parts that can be printed in nylon, across every industry in the left-hand column. Publishing a thin page for each of them would be easy and worthless — the pages would be one template with the nouns swapped. So the library is built the other way: the matrix below is planned in the open, and a cell turns into a guide only when its content can be genuinely specific to that part — its loads, its design numbers, the way it fails. Coverage so far is one industry hub deep; the map shows exactly how far it goes and what is queued behind it.</p>

  <h2>The coverage matrix</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table matrix-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Industry \ Part family</th>
          {APPLICATION_PART_FAMILIES.map((f) => <th>{f.name}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {APPLICATION_INDUSTRIES.map((ind) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              {HUB_INDUSTRIES.has(ind.slug)
                ? <a href={`/applications/${ind.slug}/`}>{ind.name}</a>
                : ind.name}
            </th>
            {APPLICATION_PART_FAMILIES.map((fam) => {
              const cell = cellFor(ind.slug, fam.slug);
              const app = cell?.status === 'published' ? appFor(ind.slug, fam.slug) : undefined;
              return (
                <td>
                  {app
                    ? <a class="status-live" href={`/applications/${ind.slug}/${app.slug}/`}>Guide published</a>
                    : cell
                      ? <span class="status-planned">Planned</span>
                      : <span class="status-none">—</span>}
                </td>
              );
            })}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">“Guide published” links to the application guide. “Planned” means the combination is in the coverage plan and waits its turn under the double gate described above — there is no page behind it yet, and this map does not pretend otherwise. A dash means the combination is not currently planned. The first hub, <a href="/applications/drones/">Drones &amp; UAV</a>, covers {APPLICATIONS.filter((a) => a.industry === 'drones').length} part families today: {APPLICATIONS.filter((a) => a.industry === 'drones').map((a) => a.title.replace(/^Drone /, '').toLowerCase()).join(' and ')}.</p>
  <p class="updated">Last updated: {SITE.lastUpdated}</p>
</Base>
